  "details": "If you're not familiar with the concept of \"Body Remapping\", here's a quick timestamped snippet of the [Bonelab reveal video](https://youtu.be/C55zPYtUflw?si=EW968uCMF3afqmOp&t=176) that shows it in action. Placing your hands on your body in real life results in your avatar doing the same action, regardless of the differences in body proportions.\n\nThe idea is essentially that the space where your in-game hands are positioned is gently warped away from your physical controller's position as they come closer to your physical body to account for how close/far your avatar's body is from your own. For example, this allows for placing your hands on your stomach while wearing an avatar with a larger frame than your own without your hands clipping through the mesh, or doing the same on a thinner avatar without your hands floating away from you. \n\nBonelab finds your real life body surfaces by allowing users to input their height and t-shirt size, but allow for an \"advanced\" input of more precise body measurements if desired. I feel like this is a pretty elegant way to go about it.\n\nExperimenting with your identity through avatars is already such a huge part of VRChat's pull and is deeply engrained in social interaction on the platform, I feel like adding something similar to Bonelab's body remapping feature is a no-brainer. It'd be so awesome for people to be able to act naturally without needing to make micro-adjustments to their movements to avoid clipping, or having to worry about their body proportions \"not being compatible\" with the avatar they'd like to use. I would really love to see this implemented as an option someday.",
